#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151849 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3601ac83-0800-567a-9a0d-4d5c37cef413" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to an Equivalence Class"}] :question/id #uuid "b2dd8c4b-cabe-5a47-b6cd-b426f41280a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14603" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{Z},$ defined by $x \\sim y$ if $12 \\,|\\, (x-y).$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[9]?$\n\nI. $9$\n\nII. $21$\n\nIII. $22$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[9]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $9$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[9] & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x∼9} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,12\\,|\\,(x-9)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x=9+12k,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-15,-3,9,21,33,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $9$ and $21$ are in $[9],$ while $22$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151851 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dacfbca6-6135-5d51-a07e-627fce79dfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/id #uuid "bc24cde3-b907-5bc0-9889-a7a54b10a2a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14605" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{R},$ defined by $x \\sim y$ if $\\sin x = \\sin y.$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}?$\n\nI. $\\left[\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n\nII. $\\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n\nIII. $\\left[-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$" :question/worked-solution "We have $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\in [x]$ if $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\sim x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\not\\in \\left[\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$ because $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) =-1\\neq 1 \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\not\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$ because $\\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) =-1\\neq 1 \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$ because $\\sin \\left(-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = 1  \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151852 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21e3832f-b11f-5501-bf39-74665b88c38b" :knowledge-point/title "Counting Equivalence Classes"}] :question/id #uuid "1e93cc7e-4adc-5a6a-94d1-966d67511a19" :question/math-academy-id "e-14606" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on the set $A = \\{0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\},$ defined by $a \\sim b$ if $\\textrm{i}^a = \\textrm{i}^b,$ where $\\textrm{i}$ is the imaginary unit in $\\mathbb{C}.$  How many distinct equivalence classes does $\\sim$ partition $A$ into?" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence classes of $\\sim$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a \\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={a\\in A∣a∼0} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}={\\text{i}}^{0}} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}=1} \\\\  & ={0,4,8} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={a\\in A∣a∼1} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}={\\text{i}}^{1}} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}=\\text{i}} \\\\  & ={1,5,9} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={a\\in A∣a∼2} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}={\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}=-1} \\\\  & ={2,6} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={a\\in A∣a∼3} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}={\\text{i}}^{3}} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}=-\\text{i}} \\\\  & ={3,7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEach element of $A$ has been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $4$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0] = \\{ 0, 4, 8 \\} , \\quad [1] = \\{ 1, 5, 9 \\}, \\quad [2] = \\{ 2, 6 \\}, \\quad [3] = \\{ 3, 7 \\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3601ac83-0800-567a-9a0d-4d5c37cef413" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to an Equivalence Class"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fb079ab-f666-5cc0-acd0-dc79af768608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421652} {:answer/id #uuid "641a1fbc-83f5-514f-b0d5-77a3f28046d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421661} {:answer/id #uuid "beda1346-455c-59a5-b6e2-690d2703d950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421684} {:answer/id #uuid "d8687927-2b83-51b2-aa18-5333115cbc1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421693} {:answer/id #uuid "e261b60f-981d-5dea-add0-42767006246e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e261b60f-981d-5dea-add0-42767006246e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421697} :answer-field/id #uuid "11f848d9-afc8-5368-b1fd-07597c4b200a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9e1ee25-17a0-56ec-9f69-bcad0ae7d0cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-221717" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[0]?$\n\nI. $2\\pi $\n\nII. $3\\pi $\n\nIII. $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[0]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $0$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1} \\\\  & ={…,-4\\pi ,-2\\pi ,0,2\\pi ,4\\pi ,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $2\\pi $ is in $[0],$ while $3\\pi $ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3601ac83-0800-567a-9a0d-4d5c37cef413" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to an Equivalence Class"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bd8b0ab-150b-54d0-81ad-62745455f13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421653} {:answer/id #uuid "5e428509-d761-5d25-9064-b0a933d1e087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421660} {:answer/id #uuid "65034044-ad36-5de4-9218-9c6031ac2efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421663} {:answer/id #uuid "a11d855a-0bb1-51c3-a321-8b28ec6504e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421677} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f156b1-f4c7-51f2-8b59-ff35f351faee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65034044-ad36-5de4-9218-9c6031ac2efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421663} :answer-field/id #uuid "9412c8de-1e86-51d7-8119-c14b13217534" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "22db406c-85ef-5a5a-ab22-d062645b4362" :question/math-academy-id "q-221718" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $7\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[5]?$\n\nI. $12$\n\nII. $-4$\n\nIII. $-2$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[5]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $5$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[5] & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x∼5} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,7\\,|\\,(x-5)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x=5+7k,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-9,-2,5,12,19,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $12$ and $-2$ are in $[5],$ while $-4$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dacfbca6-6135-5d51-a07e-627fce79dfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0870007d-28f6-5394-a3f7-191763ad0726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421634} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8f1e44-03e2-51d0-9aa1-2173d810665d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421644} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa42817-8906-5d24-8213-162156ed884a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421645} {:answer/id #uuid "5812dc9d-ae67-5387-a7a7-136b1f26b10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421658} {:answer/id #uuid "8f50ab69-dd3e-5316-98aa-d4140b7a3a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f50ab69-dd3e-5316-98aa-d4140b7a3a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421671} :answer-field/id #uuid "648ceec9-0c7f-519e-9b1b-ce3a5608f2be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d844129-f571-5c80-934e-cfc1faad53cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-221722" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $11\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $-6?$\n\nI. $[5]$\n\nII. $[-17]$\n\nIII. $[-8]$" :question/worked-solution "We have $-6\\in [x]$ if $-6∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11\\,|\\,(-6-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $-6\\in [5]$ because $11\\,|\\,(-6-5)\\,✓$\n\n- $-6\\in [-17]$ because $11\\,|\\,(-6+17)\\,✓$\n\n- $-6\\notin [-8]$ because $11∤(-6+8)\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21e3832f-b11f-5501-bf39-74665b88c38b" :knowledge-point/title "Counting Equivalence Classes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e6d699-08b0-544b-9709-1cd54a9ffc8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421632} {:answer/id #uuid "08d340e2-57be-5c32-979c-5f7afaac6925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421635} {:answer/id #uuid "14219d11-e190-51df-ac53-5ec087b97fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421638} {:answer/id #uuid "72a5fab9-0b55-533d-b336-def8d8d6a083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421666} {:answer/id #uuid "98ea4427-660c-5ec0-9b2f-57f808b3fb64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08d340e2-57be-5c32-979c-5f7afaac6925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421635} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c22446d-4a02-5dda-91e6-5dcac0568fd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f558440e-fbbe-5f40-9ac9-a9ba45c6c38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-221739" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,1,2,…,20},$ defined by $a∼b$ if $5∣(b-a).$   How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼0} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-0)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣a} \\\\  & ={0,5,10,15,20} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼1} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-1)} \\\\  & ={1,6,11,16} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼2} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-2)} \\\\  & ={2,7,12,17} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼3} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-3)} \\\\  & ={3,8,13,18} \\\\  \\\\ [4] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼4} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-4)} \\\\  & ={4,9,14,19} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $5$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0,5,10,15,20},\\,[1]={1,6,11,16},\\,[2]={2,7,12,17},\\,[3]={3,8,13,18},\\,[4]={4,9,14,19}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21e3832f-b11f-5501-bf39-74665b88c38b" :knowledge-point/title "Counting Equivalence Classes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e08c36-c324-5321-9cf4-a44ee441312d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421636} {:answer/id #uuid "934a840c-bff8-5510-9234-70b0bf17c493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421672} {:answer/id #uuid "be425907-329d-5a10-b5db-ab365fa81476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421683} {:answer/id #uuid "ca907d65-9e23-5d93-9273-436e6ef31767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421688} {:answer/id #uuid "f38111d5-9586-53b6-857b-55f2afbf3761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f38111d5-9586-53b6-857b-55f2afbf3761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421700} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8ae37cc-586d-5f42-83a1-41443720bcf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d77f9f0f-9817-528d-99c4-631fe49372a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-221743" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4},$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{2}={y}^{2}.$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in A∣x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={x\\in A∣x∼1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={1}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=1} \\\\  & ={-1,1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={x\\in A∣x∼2} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=4} \\\\  & ={-2,2} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={x\\in A∣x∼3} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={3}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=9} \\\\  & ={-3,3} \\\\  \\\\ [4] & ={x\\in A∣x∼4} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={4}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=16} \\\\  & ={-4,4} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $5$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0},\\,[1]={-1,1},\\,[2]={-2,2},\\,[3]={-3,3},\\,[4]={-4,4}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421726 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21e3832f-b11f-5501-bf39-74665b88c38b" :knowledge-point/title "Counting Equivalence Classes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246698a2-b130-53da-bda8-fec452481f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421642} {:answer/id #uuid "31fdab92-c52b-5cd9-908e-a9910f257a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421646} {:answer/id #uuid "3a330020-e0e7-502a-aae9-975b1ea142f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421649} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed6509c-082f-5a78-aa0f-ee6aed242876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421667} {:answer/id #uuid "c4575dee-b141-59aa-a6f1-d51a11bf677c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ed6509c-082f-5a78-aa0f-ee6aed242876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421667} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ae4ef16-e79e-5d40-a0c5-0776f84027eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d047cc89-db39-59a7-988f-ed7cc91aeaba" :question/math-academy-id "q-221762" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,1,2,…,9},$ defined by $a∼b$ if $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi b).$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={a\\in A∣a∼0} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=1} \\\\  & ={0,2,4,6,8} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={a\\in A∣a∼1} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=-1} \\\\  & ={1,3,5,7,9} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $2$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0,2,4,6,8},\\,[1]={1,3,5,7,9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dacfbca6-6135-5d51-a07e-627fce79dfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "209a9659-21f3-5335-86ea-8fb565a19aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421640} {:answer/id #uuid "82a555b5-7e6b-57ec-a220-0322b30bb853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421669} {:answer/id #uuid "c26909a8-bef0-5d9a-ab7f-4e353af1d2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218604549