\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21e3832f-b11f-5501-bf39-74665b88c38b" :knowledge-point/title "Counting Equivalence Classes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ae0ef5-2366-5ee7-acd1-7e7f0b4934e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421633} {:answer/id #uuid "4d03777b-1fee-5d96-86f0-9cc3083eece8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421654} {:answer/id #uuid "887d094a-8f57-58e4-81a8-5c7120e865ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421670} {:answer/id #uuid "956745eb-9ed2-5e68-a4cf-efff0a5b57e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421673} {:answer/id #uuid "9b832a45-24f1-5a5d-954e-36278af115a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07ae0ef5-2366-5ee7-acd1-7e7f0b4934e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421633} :answer-field/id #uuid "d91d4103-d958-5264-a7e2-ea19b7cddb7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67dde538-2d6b-5e8f-8a86-3af3686c7b06" :question/math-academy-id "q-221800" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4},$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{4}={y}^{4}.$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in A∣x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={0}^{4}} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={x\\in A∣x∼1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={1}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=1} \\\\  & ={-1,1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={x\\in A∣x∼2} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={2}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=16} \\\\  & ={-2,2} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={x\\in A∣x∼3} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={3}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=81} \\\\  & ={-3,3} \\\\  \\\\ [4] & ={x\\in A∣x∼4} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={4}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=256} \\\\  & ={-4,4} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $5$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0},\\,[1]={-1,1},\\,[2]={-2,2},\\,[3]={-3,3},\\,[4]={-4,4}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21e3832f-b11f-5501-bf39-74665b88c38b" :knowledge-point/title "Counting Equivalence Classes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1240daa9-b651-5f5c-bb43-9544bbf4a150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421637} {:answer/id #uuid "38cc44be-7b79-5869-84e8-72d1c10f440c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421647} {:answer/id #uuid "706cbb30-5603-57aa-9fbf-2c39c7c2d1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421664} {:answer/id #uuid "9c555a80-5092-5c33-8f5f-8b20d956b1b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421676} {:answer/id #uuid "be07dead-0e39-5015-8521-d67fa2c0e4b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c555a80-5092-5c33-8f5f-8b20d956b1b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421676} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2bb8163-4fc3-598a-b15f-be6314a5e713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd31c5ed-d4ae-58a5-ab4f-af985ca907a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-221801" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2},$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}.$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in A∣x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=0} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={x\\in A∣x∼1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=1} \\\\  & ={-1,1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={x\\in A∣x∼2} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=4} \\\\  & ={-2,2} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $3$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0},\\,[1]={-1,1},\\,[2]={-2,2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dacfbca6-6135-5d51-a07e-627fce79dfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ceda99fe-9a89-538b-92fe-b9c3561af0c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151032} {:answer/id #uuid "d631c338-a791-53b2-91c3-3159ad3deab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151033} {:answer/id #uuid "2c2719e4-e6df-5dc9-8684-b89dfbd92824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151034} {:answer/id #uuid "47f6ca37-dd85-59da-bfe2-4f7a859f36ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151035} {:answer/id #uuid "6979a5a8-b5a8-55b8-9ed4-6ea49cd616c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c2719e4-e6df-5dc9-8684-b89dfbd92824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151034} :answer-field/id #uuid "28538709-e3ec-5163-8146-0112145cee55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57bb64c1-83a8-5ad6-a9bc-3ab2aa9fabbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-76893" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $C,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{4}={y}^{4}.$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $i?$\n\nI. $[-1]$\n\nII. $[-i]$\n\nIII. $[2i]$" :question/worked-solution "We have $i\\in [x]$ if $i∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{4} & ={x}^{4} \\\\ 1 & ={x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $i\\in [-1]$ because $(-1)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\in [-i]$ because $(-i)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\notin [2i]$ because $(2i)^{4}=16\\ne 1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3601ac83-0800-567a-9a0d-4d5c37cef413" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to an Equivalence Class"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c166d58-96a2-5983-ae04-48fd94ad9bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421643} {:answer/id #uuid "543e9849-f4a4-5bbb-8761-4948634144e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421656} {:answer/id #uuid "cac53ec3-ca9a-580f-8859-c15b54c1a6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421689} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f10726-4c28-598f-8721-377b23cb1f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421691} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d0b779-1c8e-53da-9eb6-c9fdd1455bf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6d0b779-1c8e-53da-9eb6-c9fdd1455bf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421692} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe48b365-00e3-52fe-9ee6-28adef726d37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "071418af-96d7-52c9-8845-42c31b88cc12" :question/math-academy-id "q-77200" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{3}={y}^{3}.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[8]?$\n\nI. $-2$\n\nII. $2$\n\nIII. $8$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[8]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $8$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8] & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼8} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,{x}^{3}={8}^{3}} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x=8} \\\\  & ={8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $8$ is in $[8],$ while $-2$ and $2$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3601ac83-0800-567a-9a0d-4d5c37cef413" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to an Equivalence Class"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15e14667-0aa0-583f-8209-0c7938abde55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421639} {:answer/id #uuid "b90a23b8-30d5-5c66-9bec-51828ed054a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421679} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf68f06-aef2-50e7-b2b3-5af7e8f1bb85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421680} {:answer/id #uuid "c38d0ace-0856-55bb-81d0-6cb2539ca988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421686} {:answer/id #uuid "ef394b6b-c6c2-50f9-812e-b8ed02458a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c38d0ace-0856-55bb-81d0-6cb2539ca988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421686} :answer-field/id #uuid "427832c3-ff8d-5621-b856-408a0605c9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c1db003-08b1-562e-8980-51389652bb7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-77205" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $C,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{2}={y}^{2}.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[-4]?$\n\nI. $-4$\n\nII. $4$\n\nIII. $4\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[-4]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $-4$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-4] & ={x\\in C\\,|\\,x∼-4} \\\\  & ={x\\in C\\,|\\,{x}^{2}=(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in C\\,|\\,{x}^{2}=16} \\\\  & ={-4,4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-4$ and $4$ are in $[-4],$ while $4\\text{i}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dacfbca6-6135-5d51-a07e-627fce79dfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bd4c6cf-cad2-50eb-8893-138f4922e512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421650} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa231d2-67a6-5c53-87ae-7c44aba80acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421651} {:answer/id #uuid "561c2307-984b-58e7-aebc-35715b1b1d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421657} {:answer/id #uuid "5a8128d1-2683-5c3d-9347-079db2426780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421659} {:answer/id #uuid "bc38deb0-31a4-57b8-936c-ff1eb6dda150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "561c2307-984b-58e7-aebc-35715b1b1d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421657} :answer-field/id #uuid "5267f8e2-d7a6-55fe-b248-6c38530bb2a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd41a54f-8627-52be-9ff1-ad5548f86090" :question/math-academy-id "q-77214" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $3\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $5?$\n\nI. $[3]$\n\nII. $[-1]$\n\nIII. $[8]$" :question/worked-solution "We have $5\\in [x]$ if $5∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,|\\,(5-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $5\\notin [3]$ because $3∤(5-3)\\,\\times $\n\n- $5\\in [-1]$ because $3\\,|\\,(5-(-1))\\,✓$\n\n- $5\\in [8]$ because $3\\,|\\,(5-8)\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3601ac83-0800-567a-9a0d-4d5c37cef413" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to an Equivalence Class"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23beadc4-1a1f-515e-995f-8df492e565df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421641} {:answer/id #uuid "3927cc39-bdb6-50da-bb44-c5b049718f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421648} {:answer/id #uuid "6436b354-fb27-5476-8641-51d7906d4663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421662} {:answer/id #uuid "81eda3bf-1013-5fe7-84cd-f856f077edc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421668} {:answer/id #uuid "fc29ca23-eb46-59b2-b550-672eac436718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc29ca23-eb46-59b2-b550-672eac436718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421701} :answer-field/id #uuid "4af3f260-723a-5617-9400-73aac1989855" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb353c96-604c-5c65-9adf-47096b9ec124" :question/math-academy-id "q-86223" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt[3]{y}.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[-8]?$\n\nI. $-8$\n\nII. $8$\n\nIII. $2$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[-8]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $-8$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-8] & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼-8} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt[3]{-8}} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}=-2} \\\\  & ={-8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-8$ is in $[-8],$ while $8$ and $2$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dacfbca6-6135-5d51-a07e-627fce79dfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52c8fe5b-d375-581b-9b3a-52597f4673c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421655} {:answer/id #uuid "712da55b-bdaa-506a-bc71-7d0285b9be2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421665} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8e86ce-b4e2-5ece-9883-422f68961751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421690} {:answer/id #uuid "dd6864e6-1d99-5d8e-b6f4-0cdb7769034b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421696} {:answer/id #uuid "ef949561-2cb0-5e30-8ccf-97ae2720042d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd8e86ce-b4e2-5ece-9883-422f68961751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421690} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2fd66d6-e133-56c3-81e9-9ab5d15cb8ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c88f13fc-8d9b-5fc7-ac75-4d5797158af8" :question/math-academy-id "q-86231" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $2\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $9?$\n\nI. $[5]$\n\nII. $[2]$\n\nIII. $[19]$" :question/worked-solution "We have $9\\in [x]$ if $9∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,|\\,(9-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $9\\in [5]$ because $2\\,|\\,(9-5)\\,✓$\n\n- $9\\notin [2]$ because $2∤(9-2)\\,\\times $\n\n- $9\\in [19]$ because $2\\,|\\,(9-19)\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}]}
